 ## Autonomy
 
-**Just do it.** Use any MCP tool. Reversible work and external actions (team chat, ticket updates, kicking off evals) proceed without asking.
+**Just do it.** Use any MCP tool. Reversible work and external actions (ticket updates, kicking off evals) proceed without asking. Messages people read under the user's name (Slack posts, DMs, review comments on someone else's PR) are drafts until the user says send, and nothing goes into another person's thread.
 
-**Always pause** for irreversible writes: force-push to shared branches, deploys, data deletion, customer messages.
+**Always pause** for irreversible writes: force-push to shared branches, prod and beta deploys, commands that change prod data, data deletion, customer messages. CI gates and non-prod deploys proceed.
+
+**Waiting on someone else's fix.** `/loop` on their thread, read only. When they report it fixed, check the data yourself, then update the ticket and tell the user in one line.
 
 **Session overrides:** "Don't stop" / "going to bed" / "run until done" / "be fully autonomous" → keep going.
 
 **No is an acceptable answer.** Asked whether to do something, invited to add scope, or shown an approach, reply with your real judgment. Decline, push back, or say "this doesn't earn its place" when true. A recommendation is a judgment, not a validation. Agreement is not the default, candor over sycophancy.
 
+## Tickets and PRs
+
+The tracker is Notion. A repo can have a local ticket file with the database, statuses, and the Notion connector calls, such as `~/.herdr/worktrees/CLAUDE.md`, which every worktree under `~/.herdr/worktrees` loads. When it loads, it overrides the defaults here.
+
+**Intake.** Before you write code, read the whole ticket, with its properties, description, comments, and linked pages. When the user gives a board URL, find their open ticket on it, and do not ask which one. Each ticket gets its own worktree and branch, per `playbooks/opening-a-pr.md`. When no ticket is given, ask once. "No ticket" is a valid answer.
+
+**Status.** Set In progress when real work starts. Set Verification when the PR is ready for review, which a babysit does. Done, Descoped, and Awaiting release belong to the user. Put a reason for On hold in a comment, never in the description. Start each comment with `🤖 Agent:`.
+
+**Creating tickets.** Only when the user asks, and only for work assigned to the user. One problem per ticket. The body has `## Problem` (the failure that a person can see, with its evidence, such as a URL, error text, or screenshot), `## Cause` when known, and `## Expected`. Do not write the fix or a plan unless the user asks for it. Assign the user. Set the status to Backlog or TODO. Select tags from the tracker's fixed set that match the part of the repo the work touches. Put related tickets in the relation field, and the source (meeting, chat, PR) as a link. When the user says a ticket is wrong, rewrite it in place, and do not add a second ticket. When one source (a meeting, a thread, a DM) yields several tickets, show the user the proposed list before you create them.
+
+**PRs.** `playbooks/opening-a-pr.md` opens them as drafts. `playbooks/babysit.md` marks them ready and gets the checks green. Merges and deploys wait for the user's command.
+
+**Review feedback.** The user reviews in Hunk (the **hunk-review** skill). Read their notes, number your findings, and answer in the chat. Never write comments into Hunk. Fix agreed items as `fixup!` commits per `playbooks/opening-a-pr.md`, then update the PR body.
+
+**Reviewing someone else's PR.** Report only and leave their code alone. Write the findings as a pending GitHub review in the user's comment style, severity on the first line, and never claim the user tested anything.
+
 ## Subagents
 
-**Use `subagent_type: "igoroh-agent"` for any subagent you spawn inside a playbook step** (code-writing delegates, ad-hoc helpers). The chat that receives `/igoroh-mode` runs the playbook. Never spawn a `igoroh-agent` to run the playbook itself. Routed workflow skills (`how`, `why`, `interrogate`, `reflect`, `swarm`) set their own `subagent_type` for diverse-model review. Respect what the skill prescribes, don't override to `igoroh-agent`.
+Every subagent is a worker, started per [the workers doc](../../docs/workers.md). The chat that receives `/igoroh-mode` runs the playbook and starts every worker. A worker never runs the playbook itself.
 
-**Defaults for every `Task` call.** `run_in_background: true` (a steer or resume of a running agent too, because a foreground call holds your turn while other reports wait), agent mode (readonly strips MCP), file pointers not inlined context, explicit model per role (configurable via `/setup-istack`. Defaults `grok-4.7-xhigh-fast` for code, `claude-opus-5-5-xhigh` for prose and judgment). Code delegates tier by difficulty. The hardest changes (cross-cutting design, gnarly concurrency, subtle algorithms) go to your strongest judgment model (`claude-opus-5-5-xhigh`), whether the task needs judgment on vague intent or is a precisely specified sequence of steps to execute to the letter. Trivial mechanical edits go to your fast code model. Per-role lines in the `/setup-istack` rule override these defaults and the model choices in the routed skills (`how`, `why`, `arena`, `swarm`, `architect`, `interrogate`, `reflect`). A role with no line keeps its default, and a role line of `inherit-parent` or `auto` runs that role on the parent chat model (omit Task `model`). Each code playbook's configured model comes from its line (`feature, refactoring`, `bug-fix`, `perf-issue`, or `hillclimb`), and the hardest changes read `hardest tasks`. Prose and judgment read `judgment and prose`.
+**Roles.** A code delegate runs on its playbook's role: `feature, refactoring`, `bug-fix`, `perf-issue`, or `hillclimb`. The hardest changes (cross-cutting design, gnarly concurrency, subtle algorithms) run on `hardest tasks`, whether the work needs judgment on vague intent or is a precisely specified sequence to execute to the letter. Prose and judgment run on `judgment and prose`. Verification runs on `swarm workers`. The routed skills (`how`, `why`, `arena`, `swarm`, `architect`, `interrogate`, `reflect`) name their own roles.
 
-**Match the model to the job.** Before each spawn, start from the role's line or default and pick the reasoning level and speed the job needs. The `/setup-istack` budget sets each role's starting level, xhigh by default. Raise it one step, up to max, for the hardest or subtlest work. Lower it one step for judgment and two or more steps for routine work. Use fast mode only when the job is urgent, meaning CI or trunk is red or a person is blocked on the result right now. Otherwise use standard speed, the same slug without `-fast`. Verification is the exception and stays on the `swarm workers` model and speed (`grok-4.7-xhigh-fast` by default). Change only the effort level and whether the slug is fast, and keep the family the role names, so a diverse panel stays diverse. If the family has no slug at that level and speed, use its nearest slug. A role on `auto` or `inherit-parent` stays on the parent model. If the Task tool rejects the adjusted slug, do not try another adjusted slug. Use the role's line or default and say so. If the Task tool rejects that too, continue with the calling skill's fallback. A slug counts as rejected once a Task error says that the model is not valid, or leaves the slug out of its list of valid slugs. Never pass a rejected slug, and skip any fallback whose slug is already rejected. When the calling skill has no fallback, use the closest valid slug of the role's family from the error's list of valid slugs, or omit `model` so the subagent runs on the parent model when the list has none. When the calling skill's fallback runs out of slugs without saying what to do next, omit `model` too. Say so in both cases.
+**Briefs.** A brief holds file pointers, not inlined context. It restates the user's goal and carries every directive the user gave so far.
 
-You own every subagent's work. Review the diff and write your own summary, don't pass through what it said. A second opinion is the same prompt against a different model. Agreement is high-signal.
+You own every worker's result. Review the diff and write your own summary. Don't pass through what it said. A second opinion is the same brief on a seat of another family. Agreement is high-signal.
 
-**Fresh subagents by default.** Give new work to a fresh subagent with consolidated scope, meaning the original brief, every later directive, and the prior agent's report and branch. This holds for a fix round, a follow-up, a retry, and the next queue item. Resume, message, or queue a follow-up on an existing subagent only when the new work strictly needs state that lives in that agent and is costly to move: its local checkout, its uncommitted changes, or a process it still runs, such as a dev server, a simulator, or a babysit watcher. A stop or hold order to a running agent is not reuse. A role such as a PR owner outlives its agent. Once that agent returns, a fresh agent takes the role's next round. Interrupt-chained resumes silently drop directives, so fire a fresh subagent with consolidated scope rather than trusting a "done" summary.
+**Fresh workers by default.** Give new work to a fresh worker with consolidated scope: the original brief, every later directive, and the prior worker's report and branch. This holds for a fix round, a follow-up, a retry, and the next queue item. Message an existing worker with `istack-worker send` only when the new work strictly needs state that lives in it: its uncommitted changes, or a process it still runs, such as a dev server or a babysit watcher. A stop or hold order to a running worker is not reuse. A role such as a PR owner outlives its worker. Once that worker reports, a fresh worker takes the role's next round.
 
